body:
## Summary
This PR adds a C# analyzer that warns programmers when declaring a class inheriting from `GodotObject` that does not declare a parameterless constructor.
Parameterless constructors are a requirement for C# classes inheriting from `GodotObject`.  Documentation relating to it is not front-and-center (I would expect to find it under [C# API differences to GDScript][csharp-api-differences-to-gdscript], [Current Gotchas and Known Issues][current-gotchas-and-known-issues], or [Common Pitfalls][common-pitfalls]).  Crashes caused by not declaring parameterless constructors do not provide easily-diagnosable errors.

From the [official documentation][creating-your-own-resources] (first code example under the C# tab):
> Make sure you provide a parameterless constructor.
> In C#, a parameterless constructor is different from a constructor with all default values.
> Without a parameterless constructor, Godot will have problems creating and editing your resource via the inspector.

## Changes
- Add analyzer and diagnostic in `Godot.SourceGenerators`
- Add code fix provider that adds a parameterless constructor
- Add tests in `Godot.SourceGenerators.Tests`

## Example
```cs
// This will not emit a warning because the class does not inherit from `GodotObject`
class MyObject1 {
  public MyObject1(int value) { /* ... */ }
}

// This will not emit a warning because the class does not declare any constructors
partial class MyObject2 : Node { }

// This will not emit a warning because the class declares a parameterless constructor
partial class MyObject3 : Node {
  public MyObject3() { /* ... */ }
}

// This will not emit a warning because the class declares a parameterless constructor
partial class MyObject4 : Node {
  public MyObject4() { /* ... */ }

  public MyObject4(int value) { /* ... */ }
}

// This WILL emit a warning because the class does not declare a parameterless constructor
partial class MyObject5 : Node {
  public MyObject5(int value) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Concerns
- I gave the diagnostic the ID `GD0004`, since it is related to class declarations like `GD0001` - `GD0003`.
There may be a more appropriate ID to give it.
- I made the diagnostic a warning, not an error like all other analyzers.
I can't imagine a situation where this *isn't* an error, but there may be a legitimate use that I don't know about.

